    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d299">

                <pb facs="0400" n="388" sameAs="frus:frus1946v08/pg388"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00116 PW/1–1346: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Kennan</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-01-13T13:00:00+03:00">January 13, 1946—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 4:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>127. ReEmbs 123, Jan 12, 3[<hi rendition="#italic">1?</hi>] p.m. At Lozovski’s
                    invitation I called on him yesterday evening at FornOff.</p>
                <p>He referred to our invitation to participate in trial of Japanese war criminals
                    and said that Sov Govt was not adequately
                    informed about this trial. They wished to have copy of indictment and also a
                    list of leading criminals. Furthermore, they were unable to understand position
                    of George [<hi rendition="#italic">Joseph</hi>] B. Keenan, as described to them
                    in Dept’s note of Jan [3?]<anchor n="95"
                        xml:id="d299-f1"/> to SovEmb Washington. This note described him, <pb
                        facs="0401" n="389" xml:id="pg389"/> in translation received by FornOff
                    here, as Chief of the International Section of Prosecuting Attorneys. What body
                    was this? By whom established? And who, exactly, was Mr. Keenan? Was he an
                    official of United States Government?</p>
                <p>I undertook to obtain this information for him as soon as possible and would
                    appreciate early instructions.</p>
                <p>May I point out that Russian interest in details of indictment and of list of
                    criminals is more than formal. Russians will not have failed to note press
                    stories such as one which recently appeared in certain American papers
                    concerning document allegedly published by Konoye’s son on Jap-Russian agreement for division of Asia; and
                    they will no doubt wish to make certain that no such document, or indeed any
                    evidence mentioning Matsuoka’s 1941 Moscow talks<anchor n="96" xml:id="d299-f2"
                    /> and resultant Neutrality Pact<anchor n="97" xml:id="d299-f3"/> or other
                        Jap-Russian exchanges, is adduced in trial
                    by any party in connection with Jap plans for
                    aggression.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kennan</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d299-f1" n="95" xml:id="d299-f1-content">
                <p>See note of December 28, 1945, to the Australian Legation, <ref
                        target="frus1945v06#pg_988"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>,
                        1945, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">vi</hi>, p. 988</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d299-f2" n="96" xml:id="d299-f2-content">
                <p>Yosuke Matsuoka, Japanese Minister for Foreign Affairs, visited Moscow en route
                    to and from his trip to Germany; for reports of his mission, see <ref
                        target="frus1941v04#pg_915"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>,
                        1941, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">iv</hi>, pp. 915</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d299-f3" n="97" xml:id="d299-f3-content">
                <p>Signed at Moscow, April 13, 1941; for text, see Department of State <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, April 29, 1945, p. 811. Cf. telegram 763,
                    April 13, 1941, 11 p.m., from Moscow, <ref target="frus1941v04#pg_944"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1941, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">iv</hi>, p. 944</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
